---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
export const prerender = true;
const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Tape"
  description="Tape is a tool for writing about the economy: shareable, citation-ready dashboards composed from public data."
>
  <article class="about-root">
    <p class="about-eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>A tool for writing about the economy.</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      It's Sunday night. A principal needs CPI and PCE on the same
      axes, going back to the 80s, by 8 a.m. Tape gets you from
      that ask to a chart you can paste into a memo — with a citation,
      a last-updated date, and a URL a colleague can open and modify.
    </p>

    <p>
      The library covers US macro, all 435 congressional districts,
      OECD cross-country series, federal spending by state and
      district, the equity market by sector and ticker, crypto,
      foreign Treasury holdings, and ~1,800 other series. Every
      chart shows both the current level and the move over a window
      you pick. Every source links back to its provider. Every
      dashboard is a URL.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can pull a single chart, compose several into a dashboard,
      derive new series by dividing or summing existing ones (CA
      federal dollars per dollar of US GDP; oil priced in gold),
      and share the whole thing by copying the address bar. Saved
      dashboards live under your account; everything else works
      without signing in.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>FRED</strong> (St. Louis Fed) — US Treasury yields,
        inflation, employment, GDP, federal spending by function,
        most US-macro series.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>BLS</strong> — state-level unemployment + payrolls for
        all 50 states and DC; productivity; CPI subcomponents.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>USAspending.gov</strong> — federal contracts, grants,
        loans, and direct payments by recipient state and by congressional
        district, fiscal-year, back to FY2008.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Census Bureau</strong> — American Community Survey 5-year
        estimates for income, population, poverty, and education at the
        congressional-district level.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>OECD</strong> — harmonized cross-country comparisons of
        unemployment, CPI, government debt and deficit, life expectancy,
        and health spending.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>US Treasury</strong> — TIC system foreign-holder data for
        US Treasury securities.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>World Bank</strong> — country GDP series.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Yahoo Finance</strong> (via <code>yfinance</code>) — equity
        tickers, ETFs, futures, FX, crypto spot prices.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each chart's expanded view lists exactly which sources it draws on
      and when each was last updated. Click <em>Copy citation</em> from
      any chart to get a citation block you can paste into a footnote.
    </p>
    <p>
      Want a feed we don't pull yet, or a feature we haven't built?
      <a href={`${baseUrl}/enterprise/`}>Enterprise support</a> covers
      custom data sources, private dashboards, one-click Excel export,
      and uptime commitments for teams whose work depends on the site.
    </p>

    <h2>How fresh is the data</h2>
    <p>
      Data pipelines run weekly via GitHub Actions; results are
      committed into a public repository alongside a dated snapshot
      (<code>public/data/_archive/&lt;YYYY-MM-DD&gt;/</code>), so a
      footnote citing a specific date can still resolve that vintage
      indefinitely. Series can be revised, restated, or deprecated by
      their providers; the site reflects whatever was current on the
      most recent refresh.
    </p>

    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Astro 5 (hybrid SSR) on the front end, Observable Plot for charts,
      Python pipelines for data, Vercel for hosting, Supabase (Postgres
      + Auth) for saved dashboards, PostHog for custom-event analytics.
      <a href="https://github.com/khs/tape" rel="noopener">
        Source on GitHub
      </a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Built by</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://kellerscholl.com" rel="noopener">Keller Scholl</a>,
      based in DC. Started this because nobody else seemed to be doing it
      and I kept needing it.
      Notes, corrections, and suggestions at
      <a href="mailto:keller.scholl@gmail.com">keller.scholl@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="recent">Recent additions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Choropleth maps.</strong> ACS demographic indicators at
        state, county, census-tract, and block-group resolution — poverty
        rate, median household income, bachelor's-degree share, and
        foreign-born share. Click any tile for an expanded view with
        mouse-wheel zoom (Ctrl+/-/0 for keyboard), a year slider through
        2010-2022 vintages, color-scheme picker, and real fullscreen.
        Featured on the <a href={`${baseUrl}/va-08/`}>VA-08</a> and
        <a href={`${baseUrl}/federal-budget/`}>Federal-budget</a>
        dashboards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Federal-budget dashboard.</strong> Outlays / receipts /
        deficit / debt as % of GDP, spending by function (entitlements,
        defense, interest), where the money lands geographically, and
        external counterparts (foreign Treasury holdings, trade balance).
        Plus the four US-wide demographic heatmaps for context.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inflation deep-dive.</strong> Headline measures (CPI,
        PCE, headline + core for both) and the components that drive
        them: food, energy, shelter, rent, medical care. The "what the
        bond market expects" section uses 5y + 10y breakevens.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Labor + Rates + Recession-monitor + Housing dashboards.</strong>
        Surfaces previously-library-only charts on dedicated pages with
        framing essays. Recession-monitor includes NY Fed's term-
        structure model probability and the Senior Loan Officer Survey
        tightening series.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="disclaimers">Legal disclaimers</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Not investment advice.</strong> Everything on this site is
      published for informational and educational purposes only. Nothing
      here is investment, financial, tax, legal, or trading advice, and
      nothing should be construed as a recommendation to buy, sell, or
      hold any security or other financial instrument. Market and
      economic data can be revised, delayed, restated, or simply wrong;
      reflected values are whatever the upstream provider returned at
      the most recent data refresh and may not be current. Do your own
      research, and consult a qualified professional before making any
      financial decision. The site, its data providers, and its author
      make no warranties of accuracy, completeness, timeliness, or
      fitness for any particular purpose, and accept no liability for
      losses arising from use of this information.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Privacy &amp; analytics.</strong> The site uses Vercel Web
      Analytics for cookieless pageview counting (no IP storage, no
      fingerprinting, no cross-site tracking) and PostHog for custom
      product events (e.g. "a dashboard was saved", "a chart was
      expanded"). PostHog autocapture is enabled, which records clicks
      and form interactions with the same data-minimization defaults
      (no third-party trackers). If you create an account, your
      Google-provided email is stored in our Supabase database solely
      so we can attach your saved dashboards to you; we don't sell,
      share, or market against this information. You can delete your
      account and all associated saves at any time by emailing
      <a href="mailto:keller.scholl@gmail.com">keller.scholl@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Data licensing.</strong> Underlying series carry their
      providers' licensing terms: FRED, BLS, USAspending.gov, Census
      Bureau, and US Treasury data are US public domain; World Bank
      Open Data is CC BY-4.0; OECD data is used under OECD terms;
      Yahoo Finance is used under each provider's published terms of
      service. Charts and dashboards composed from these sources
      inherit those terms; please credit the underlying provider when
      reproducing data elsewhere.
    </p>

    <p class="footer-note">
      <a href={baseUrl || "/"}>&larr; All dashboards</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-root {
    max-width: 680px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 64px 24px 96px;
    color: var(--color-neutral-900);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .about-eyebrow {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--color-neutral-500);
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .about-root h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }
  .about-root h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 40px 0 8px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-neutral-500);
  }
  .about-root p,
  .about-root ul {
    margin: 12px 0;
    color: var(--color-neutral-700);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
  }
  .about-root ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  .about-root li {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }
  .about-root .lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-neutral-800);
    margin-top: 0;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .about-root strong {
    color: var(--color-neutral-900);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .about-root em {
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .about-root code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    background: var(--color-neutral-100);
    padding: 1px 4px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }
  .about-root a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .footer-note {
    margin-top: 48px;
    padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(55, 53, 47, 0.08);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
